What are the 2 pros of ReLU?
The ReLU function is non-saturating, what does this mean?

The main advantage of the ReLU function is that it can greatly reduce the computational cost of training deep neural networks. Unlike the sigmoid function, the ReLU function is computationally efficient, as it does not involve any exponential operations. In addition, the ReLU function is non-saturating, which means that it does not suffer from the vanishing gradient problem that is commonly encountered when training deep neural networks with sigmoid or hyperbolic tangent (tanh) activation functions.

What are some cons of ReLU?
However, the ReLU function also has some limitations. For example, when the input is negative, it will output zero. This causes neurons that use the ReLU function as an activation function to die and stop responding to the input. This is called the "dying ReLU" problem. To mitigate this problem, some variants of the ReLU function have been proposed, such as the Leaky ReLU and the Parametric ReLU.
